LESSONS = [
L("Chapter 1 — The system is read before it is felt", "12", "<p>Every platform problem the firm ever FEELS — the slow screen, the missed reminder, the invoice that never sent, the morning the site is down — existed as a READABLE signal first: the error that started clustering last week, the scheduler that stopped on Tuesday, the queue that began deepening, the storage line that bent. The module's thesis is the track's seventh law: <b>the system is read before it is felt — because the read costs minutes and the felt costs mornings.</b></p><p><b>The gross-reads pattern, at the platform layer.</b> The academy's oldest diagnostic instinct, arriving home: every functional track taught its function to read gross signals before drilling (the aging's shape before the account, the variance's line before the row) — and the platform has its own gross reads, which this module builds into the admin's working instrument: the ERROR log's shape (Chapter 2), the SCHEDULER's heartbeat (3), the LOGIN patterns (4), the STORAGE line (5), and the PERFORMANCE feel made measurable (6) — assembled into the health page (7) that makes the platform's condition a FACT the admin states rather than a feeling the firm debates. The posture's economics, restated from module 1 and now fully priced: the daily glance is five minutes; the weekly read is fifteen; the monthly assembly is thirty — an hour a week, total, against the alternative every un-watched site eventually pays: the problem that incubated readable for weeks and detonated felt on the worst morning available.</p><p><b>The reading discipline.</b> The epistemics, imported whole from six tracks and sharpened for machines: TREND before level (the error count of forty means nothing; the error count that was four last month means everything — every read in this module is a line, not a number), SHAPE before detail (the glance reads silhouettes — the log's size, the queue's depth, the login map's density: the anomalous silhouette earns the drill-down; the normal one earns a tick and a closed tab — the admin who reads every log line daily has confused diligence with method and will stop within a month), DECOMPOSE before concluding (the anomaly opened by its parts — which error, which job, which user, which report — before any story is told about it), and ROUTE before fixing (the module's standing split, one more time: the reads are the admin's; the findings belong to their owners — the error in a customisation to the change register's owner, the login anomaly to the conduct layer, the slow report to its function's design conversation, the capacity signal to the boundary: the monitoring desk is a triage desk, and the admin who fixes everything they read has quietly collected every function's keys, which module 8 will not allow).</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — The error log: the site's own complaint channel", "13", "<p>The site complains in one place: the ERROR log — every failure, exception, and refusal recorded as it happens. The admin who reads it holds the site's own account of its troubles. The error chapter.</p><p><b>Reading for shape.</b> The method before the content: the log read as a SHAPE first (the daily glance's question is not 'what does each error say?' but 'what does the log look like?' — its size against yesterday's, its growth rate, its clustering: the quiet log is the tick; the log that doubled overnight is the day's first question, opened before any user feels what caused it), the shape's anatomy taught: the BASELINE known (every site has its noise floor — the known, benign, recurring entries the admin has previously triaged and named: the baseline documented in the site book so the glance compares against knowledge, not memory, and so the next admin inherits the floor instead of re-triaging it), the NEW SPECIES flagged (the error type never seen before is always worth a read — new species are how changes announce their side effects: the post-update morning's log being read with Chapter 2 of the change module's walk exactly because new species cluster there), and the CLUSTER read (one error once is weather; the same error forty times since Tuesday is a system saying something specific — the cluster's timestamp pattern telling the story: the burst at month-end is a load story; the steady drum since a deploy date is a change story; the cluster tied to one user is a training or permission story).</p><p><b>The triage.</b> The findings routed by kind, per the module's split: the error in a CUSTOMISATION's territory (the custom script's exception, the format's rendering failure — routed to the change register's entry and its owner: the customisation debt of module 5 announcing an interest payment), the error post-UPDATE (the timing read against the update log — the correlation routed to the boundary's ticket with module 1's hygiene: the log excerpt, the timestamps, the affected flow), the error in a FUNCTION's flow (the validation failing on real work — often a design or training finding for the owning function: the error log as the functions' own workaround detector, because the users who hit walls generate errors before they generate complaints), and the INFRASTRUCTURE-scented error (the timeout, the connection failure — the boundary's side, ticketed with the evidence attached), with the triage's discipline: the log worked to a KNOWN state (every entry either baseline-known, routed, or resolved — the unread error log of the module's indictment being the complaint channel nobody answers: the site that has been reporting its disease daily to an empty room).</p><p><b>The rhythm.</b> The error read's calendar: the DAILY shape glance (the silhouette, thirty seconds), the WEEKLY triage (the new species named, the clusters opened, the routings made — the fifteen minutes that keeps the log a known quantity), and the MONTHLY trend (the error count's line on the health page — falling as the site matures, and every sustained rise carrying a name: the new customisation bedding in, the update's wake, the growing branch's load: the trend that cannot be explained being itself a finding). The chapter's closing frame: the error log is the cheapest consultant the firm employs — it reports every problem, in writing, timestamped, the moment it happens, for free — and the certified admin is simply the person who finally reads its reports.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — The scheduler: every calendar's heartbeat", "13", "<p>Beneath every rhythm this academy built — the reminders, the recurring documents, the auto-emails, the queue's pump — runs one machine: the SCHEDULER and its background jobs. When it stops, every calendar stops, silently. The heartbeat chapter.</p><p><b>What rides the heartbeat.</b> The dependency map, drawn so the stakes are visible: the scheduled jobs carry the REMINDER engines (the expiry alerts of the HR track, the renewal warnings, the approval nudges — module 6 of this track's notification rules all fire FROM here), the RECURRING documents (the auto-generated entries, the standing orders), the EMAIL queue's pump (Chapter 3 of module 6's ledger moves because a job moves it — the stuck pump being the invoice-that-never-sent disease's actual mechanism), the INTEGRATION synchronisations (the doors' scheduled exchanges), and the MAINTENANCE cycles (the log rotations, the cleanup jobs) — which yields the chapter's central sentence: <b>the scheduler's failure is every calendar's silent failure at once</b> — nothing errors visibly, nothing complains, the site works perfectly for every interactive purpose, and the firm's automated nervous system is simply not firing: the licence expiry that never alerted, the recurring invoice that never generated, discovered weeks later by their consequences.</p><p><b>The reads.</b> The heartbeat watched: the SCHEDULER's status confirmed on the daily glance (running, and recently — the last-execution timestamps read: the scheduler that last fired hours ago on a minutes-cadence site IS the finding, before any consequence surfaces), the JOB list's health (the failed jobs read like the error log — by shape and cluster: the one job failing repeatedly is a specific disease; jobs failing broadly is the machinery's), the QUEUE depth (the pending-jobs count as a silhouette — the deepening queue meaning the machinery is falling behind its work: the depth that grows through the day and clears at night is load; the depth that only grows is a stall), and the LONG-RUNNER read (the job that used to take minutes taking hours — the growth story of Chapter 5 arriving at the job layer, and the candidate for the quiet-window rescheduling conversation).</p><p><b>The stopped-heartbeat procedure.</b> The finding's runbook: the stall confirmed (status, timestamps, queue depth — the three-line diagnosis), the RESTART path exercised per the boundary (the site-level restart within the admin's reach through the managed relationship's provided controls, or the ticket with the diagnosis attached — the boundary map's page answering which, in advance, because the stalled scheduler at 8 a.m. is not when to research the question), the BACKLOG's handling decided deliberately (the queue that accumulated during the stall — released to run, watched: the hundred pent reminders arriving at once being module 6's flood in miniature, and the known-stall's backlog sometimes pruned deliberately with the owning functions), and the stall LOGGED with its cause when found (the stall history being a trend of its own — the recurring stall is a capacity or defect conversation with the boundary, evidenced by its log). The chapter's closing tie: module 6's test-fire calendar (the notification rules verified quarterly) is this chapter's insurance — the heartbeat watched from above by the glance AND proven from below by the test-fires, the two directions meeting in the middle exactly as the backup layer's logs-and-drills did: the machinery proven backward and forward, the academy's standing pattern for everything invisible.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — Login & access reads: the security glance", "13", "<p>The site's doors are used all day — and the usage writes a record the admin reads: the login patterns, the failures, the anomalies. The security glance, seated between module 2's fences and module 8's incident thinking. The access chapter.</p><p><b>The reads.</b> The access layer's silhouettes: the FAILED-LOGIN read (the shape first — the scattered singles are typos and Mondays; the CLUSTER against one account is someone guessing at a door, and the cluster across many accounts is someone guessing at the site: the two clusters carrying different weights and the same instruction — read, evidence, route), the ODD-HOUR and odd-place read (the login at 3 a.m. from an unfamiliar network — sometimes the MD abroad, sometimes not: the anomaly's job is to earn a question, not an accusation — the verification conversation quiet and standard: 'was this you?' asked as routine hygiene, normalised so it alarms nobody and skips nobody), the CONCURRENT-session awareness (the account active from two places at once — the shared-password signature, module 2's one-person-one-account law being violated in the pattern before anyone admits it in words), and the DORMANT scan (module 2's monthly read, seated here on the calendar — the account unused for the horizon flagged to the lifecycle's reconciliation).</p><p><b>The evidence discipline.</b> The access reads' special standing: unlike the error log's mechanical entries, access anomalies touch PEOPLE — so the discipline tightens: EVIDENCE before conversation (the pattern captured — timestamps, sources, the account's history — before any question is asked: the question with its evidence is hygiene; the question without it is an accusation), INVESTIGATION before conclusion (the odd login has a dozen innocent explanations, and the academy's oldest fairness law — the payroll track's investigation-before-conclusion, the HR track's heard-before-judged — applies to access exactly: the pattern is a fact; the story about the pattern waits), and ROUTING by weight (the confirmed innocent anomaly logged and closed; the unexplained one escalated to module 8's incident thinking — the admin's job ending at the evidence's edge: the CONDUCT questions belong to the people layer, with the platform supplying facts, never verdicts — the boundary between 'this account did X' and 'this person did X' being real, stated, and held, because accounts are shared, sessions are left open, and the admin who skips from log line to accusation has exceeded both their evidence and their office).</p><p><b>The access log sampling.</b> The deeper record, read on rhythm: the activity trails (who touched what, when — the audit layer module 8 governs) SAMPLED periodically rather than monitored continuously (the monthly sample of sensitive-tier access — the pay-bearing doctypes' access log read for its population: the readers being who the tier design expects, the volumes being work-shaped — the sample that finds an unexpected reader routing straight to module 2's shouldn't-see procedure with its was-it-used discipline), and the sampling's honesty about its own limits (the sample detects patterns, not everything — which is exactly why module 2 built preventive fences and module 8 builds the conduct standard: the reads are one layer of a defence, never the defence). The chapter's closing frame: the access glance is the firm's cheapest security team — five minutes of silhouettes daily, the evidence discipline when a silhouette earns it, and the humility to route people-questions to people-processes: the platform watches doors; it does not judge the people who walk through them.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — Storage & growth: the site's waistline", "13", "<p>The site only grows — every document, attachment, log line, and version adds to a line that never naturally bends down. The line watched monthly, its bends explained. The growth chapter.</p><p><b>The trend read.</b> The waistline's discipline: the STORAGE figure read monthly onto its line (the number itself meaning little; the line meaning everything — the steady slope is the firm trading; the BEND is the question: the slope that doubled has a cause with a name, found by decomposition), the growth DECOMPOSED when it bends (what grew — the database or the files? which doctype's volume, which attachment class? — the platform's own size breakdowns read the way the accounts track read variance: the biggest mover first), and the growth's stories told honestly: the GOOD growth (the trading volume rising, the new branch's documents — the firm's success, showing up as bytes: budgeted for, welcomed, and priced into the boundary's capacity conversation ahead of need), and the MISUSE growth (below).</p><p><b>The misuse patterns.</b> The bends with bad causes, catalogued from the trade's reality: the BACKUP-INSIDE-THE-SITE habit (the exported spreadsheets and document dumps re-attached to records 'for safekeeping' — the site storing copies of itself: the habit read as the trust signal it is — someone doesn't believe the system keeps things, and the cure is module 4's evidence shown, not just the files removed), the WRONG-CLASS attachments (the site as a general file server — the videos, the personal archives, the whole-folder uploads riding the attachment door: the attachment policy stated and the outliers conversed with — the storage read naming its heaviest attachers not for shame but for the design conversation: what are they actually trying to do, and where should that live?), the LOG and version bloat (the technical residue growing past its useful horizon — the retention conversation of module 4 applied to the site's own exhaust, with the adviser-confirmed horizons governing what may age), and the DUPLICATE loads (module 3's import residue showing up as bulk — the botched load's remnants found by size as well as by count).</p><p><b>The capacity conversation.</b> The boundary's side, engaged ahead of need: the site's plan and its headroom KNOWN (the current usage against the arrangement's capacity — the number in the site book, refreshed at the monthly read: the capacity surprise being entirely a failure to read a line that was public all along), the growth PROJECTED simply (the slope extended — the twelve-month arrival at the ceiling foreseeable a year out: the upgrade conversation held calm and budgeted, not urgent and reactive), and the ARCHIVE conversation routed properly when the line demands it (module 4's records-retention discipline — the functions and the adviser deciding what may age out, never the admin's storage tidiness: the chapter re-stating the standing law because storage pressure is exactly when admins are tempted to break it — the waistline is managed by governance, not by deletion). The chapter's closing economics: the monthly storage read is two minutes and a dot on a line — against the site that hits its ceiling mid-quarter, uploads failing at the counter, the upgrade negotiated under duress, and the archaeology of 'what is all this?' performed in a panic that the line had been announcing for a year.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — Performance: the slow-screen craft", "14", "<p>'The system is slow' — the trade's commonest platform complaint, and the least diagnosed. This chapter is the craft that converts the feeling into a finding, at the site admin's altitude. The performance chapter.</p><p><b>Felt versus measured.</b> The discipline first: slowness is a FEELING until it is measured — the complaint met with the diagnostic questions (WHAT is slow — one screen, one report, or everything? WHEN — always, or at month-end's peak? For WHOM — one user, one branch, or all?), because the three answers route to entirely different findings (the one-screen slowness is that screen's story; the everyone-always slowness is a capacity conversation; the month-end slowness is a load pattern; the one-branch slowness is that branch's network before it is anyone's site), and the measurement habit installed: the slow thing TIMED (the report's duration noted, reproduced, recorded — the number that turns 'it's slow' into 'this report takes four minutes at 9 a.m. and forty seconds at 2 p.m.', which is a diagnosis half-made by its own phrasing).</p><p><b>The usual suspects, at admin altitude.</b> The site-level causes the admin can actually address — the honest scope (the deeper layers belong to the boundary and the partner; the admin's terrain is the site's own habits, which cause most of the felt slowness at trade scale): the HEAVY REPORT (the report over years of unfiltered data — the commonest suspect: the report designed in year one, querying everything since, slower every month by the site's own growth — the cure being the FILTER discipline below), the UNFILTERED LIST (the list view loading an entire doctype's history because its default filters were never set — the screen that opens 'slow' opening, in fact, everything), the PEAK-HOUR pile-up (the heavy reads scheduled against the counter's rush — the month-end report that everyone runs at 9 a.m. on the 1st: the scheduling conversation, and the quiet-window habit for the heavy work), the COLUMN and length habits (the report surfacing forty columns because nobody pruned it — the width carried on every row of every run), and the DEVICE and network reality named honestly (the site is only as fast as the slowest link the user sits behind — the branch on a struggling connection feels 'system slowness' that no site-side cure will fix: the network's half of the diagnosis asked early, because it is the commonest false accusation against the platform).</p><p><b>The report discipline.</b> The craft's centrepiece, because reports are where growth meets habit: reports run FILTERED (the date range mandatory in practice — the report's default period set to the useful window, not to 'everything': the saved report that queries five years for a question about last month is the site taxing itself), the DEFAULTS governed (the shared report's saved filters being configuration — set deliberately, per module 5's small-change ceremony, because the default everyone inherits is a policy), the HEAVY legitimate reads scheduled (the genuine full-history analysis run in the quiet window, welcomed there — the discipline is placement, not prohibition), and the report inventory's slow list maintained (the known-heavy reports named in the site book with their windows — the health page's performance line fed by the list's behaviour over time).</p><p><b>The routing honesty.</b> The chapter's boundary discipline: the admin's craft exhausts at the site's habits — and the slowness that survives the habits' repair routes honestly: the CAPACITY conversation with the boundary (the site that is slow with clean habits and growing load — the evidence assembled: the timings, the growth line, the peak patterns: module 1's ticket hygiene producing the capacity case in one round), and the DESIGN conversation with the partner where customisation is implicated (the custom report or script that grew slow — module 5's owned-forever debt presenting its performance invoice), with the admin's value in both being the DIAGNOSIS (the certified admin does not fix the deep layers — the certified admin arrives at them with the finding pre-made, which is the difference between 'it's slow, help' and a one-round resolution). The chapter's closing reframe: performance is a governance topic wearing a technical costume — most trade-scale slowness is habits, growth, and scheduling, all of which are readable, ownable, and cheap to fix — and the site that 'got slow' almost always got unread first.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — The health page & escalation thresholds", "13", "<p>The module's reads assembled into its deliverable: the HEALTH PAGE — the platform's condition as one weekly page of facts — and the thresholds that turn readings into actions. The assembly chapter.</p><p><b>The page's lines.</b> The weekly assembly, line by line — each a silhouette with its trend: the BACKUP line (module 4's weekly glance — cycles confirmed, sizes trended, the last drill's date standing), the ERROR line (the count against baseline, new species noted, the routings open), the HEARTBEAT line (scheduler status, failed-job count, queue depth's shape), the ACCESS line (failed-login shape, anomalies open, the dormant scan's last result), the STORAGE line (the waistline's dot, the headroom figure), the PERFORMANCE line (the slow list's behaviour, complaints logged versus measured), the OUTBOUND line (the email queue's failure count, module 6's ledger), and the DOORS line (the integrations' reconciliations current, rejection queues at zero or owned) — eight lines, each already read during the week, the page being the ASSEMBLY of glances, not new work: fifteen minutes on Friday, the platform's week stated as facts.</p><p><b>The thresholds.</b> The judgment mechanised — when a reading becomes an action: the page's each line carrying its ESCALATION thresholds, decided in calm and written down (the error count that doubles week-over-week opens a triage; the scheduler stalled twice in a month opens a boundary conversation; the storage headroom under the horizon opens the capacity case; the failed-login cluster past the line opens module 8's incident path — the thresholds being the page's difference from a diary: the diary records; the instrument ACTS, and the pre-written threshold removes the daily re-litigation of 'is this bad enough yet?', which is exactly the decision that drifts when made fresh each time under workload), and the threshold's discipline honest: thresholds REVIEWED annually (the site that grew re-prices its normals — last year's alarming queue depth is this year's Tuesday), and the threshold-crossing LOGGED with its action (the health page's history being the site's medical record — the recurring crossing being itself a chronic finding).</p><p><b>The page's readers.</b> The audiences served: the ADMIN (the working instrument — the week held in one view, the drift visible before it is felt), the COVERAGE and successor (the absence test's platform edition — the health page's history being the fastest possible orientation: the site's normals, its known issues, its trends, readable in an hour), the BOUNDARY (the capacity and defect conversations armed with the page's evidence — the trend line being the ticket's strongest attachment), and the OWNER (the quarterly platform summary — the MD's 'is the system healthy?' answered the way this academy answers everything: by pointing — the four-line quarterly digest: backups drilled and clean, errors trending down, capacity headroom at X months, zero open incidents — the platform's condition a FACT on a page, which is this module's entire thesis delivered as a deliverable).</p><p><b>The page's law.</b> The chapter's closing cadence, the module's economics stated once more: every line on the health page is minutes; every line's absence is an incubation — the un-watched site does not have fewer problems, it has later ones, discovered by feel on the mornings least convenient — and the certified admin's whole monitoring craft compresses into the sentence the track's seventh law states: the system is read before it is felt, weekly, on one page, with thresholds that act.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Case study: HealthTrade's watched year", "16", "<p>The reads installed, the page assembled, the year watched. Every chapter clocks in.</p><p><b>The heartbeat catch (Ch. 3).</b> Month two delivers the module's signature save: the daily glance finds the scheduler's last-execution timestamp six hours stale — the stall confirmed in three lines (status, timestamps, queue depth), the restart path exercised per the boundary's provided controls, the backlog released and watched (ninety pent notifications reviewed with the functions before release — the flood-in-miniature pruned of the stale, the licence-expiry alerts among the released: the alert that mattered arriving one day late instead of never). The counterfactual is written into the log deliberately: <i>unwatched, this stall runs until someone misses a reminder that matters — the pharmacist licence alert in this queue would have surfaced at month three as a compliance lapse. Caught at day two as a restart.</i></p><p><b>The access read's find (Ch. 4).</b> Quarter two: the concurrent-session silhouette flags one account active from two branches simultaneously, repeatedly — the evidence assembled first (timestamps, sources, a fortnight's pattern), the routine verification conversation held ('was this you?' — it was not, exactly: the till supervisor had shared credentials with a relief cover 'to save the request delay'), and the finding routed by weight: the shared-password violation to module 2's machinery (the relief cover granted their OWN account through the channel in a day — the delay excuse dissolved by the SLA being real), the conduct conversation to the people layer with the platform's facts and no verdicts, and the pattern logged. The deeper fix is the module's split honoured: the admin surfaced a fact; the function owned the conversation; and the request channel's speed — not a lecture — retired the workaround's reason.</p><p><b>The waistline and the slow report (Ch. 5-6).</b> The storage line bends in month six — decomposed in ten minutes: the files layer, one branch, one habit (a supervisor re-attaching weekly stock exports 'for safekeeping' — the backup-inside-the-site pattern, met as designed: the module-4 evidence shown, the restore drill's log walked through, the habit retired by trust rather than prohibition, the attachments archived by ceremony). The performance craft earns its keep in month eight: the month-end stock valuation report measured at four minutes and worsening — the diagnosis at admin altitude (unfiltered since implementation, querying every movement since go-live, run by three people at 9 a.m. on the 1st), the cure entirely within the craft (the default period set to the useful window through the small-change ceremony, the full-history version scheduled to the quiet window, the report pruned from forty columns to the eleven its readers used) — twelve seconds at the next month-end, and the finding's note quotes the chapter: <i>the site didn't get slow; it got unread. Four minutes of that report was five years of growth nobody re-filtered.</i></p><p><b>The page's year (Ch. 2, 7).</b> The health page's fifty-two Fridays close the case: the error baseline documented in month one (eleven known-benign species named — the next admin inherits the floor), the trend lines maturing (errors down as the change ceremony beds in; the storage slope re-priced after the archive pass; two threshold crossings all year, both acted within their windows), and the quarterly owner's digest landing exactly as designed — the MD's year-note entering the site book: <i>I used to ask 'is the system okay?' and receive a mood. Now I receive four lines and a trend. The difference is the whole module.</i></p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Common mistakes & the seventh law", "13", "<p>The monitoring layer's scar tissue. Symptom → disease → fix.</p><p><b>Pattern 1 — the felt-first firm.</b> Symptom: every platform problem announced by its consequences; the missed reminder discovered as a lapsed licence; the site's condition a debate of moods. Disease: no reads at all (Ch. 1). Fix: the glance, the weekly triage, the monthly assembly — an hour a week against the mornings.</p><p><b>Pattern 2 — the unread error log.</b> Symptom: the site reporting its disease daily to an empty room; the cluster that drummed for weeks before the failure everyone felt. Disease: the complaint channel nobody answers (Ch. 2). Fix: shape daily, triage weekly, baseline documented, every entry known, routed, or resolved.</p><p><b>Pattern 3 — the silent scheduler.</b> Symptom: every calendar stopped at once and nothing visibly wrong; the reminder engines dark for weeks; the consequences surfacing as compliance lapses. Disease: the heartbeat unwatched (Ch. 3). Fix: the status and timestamps on the daily glance, the test-fire calendar proving from below, the stall runbook with its boundary path pre-answered.</p><p><b>Pattern 4 — the unwatched doors.</b> Symptom: the failed-login cluster nobody saw; the shared password living in the concurrent sessions; the anomaly that was a fact for weeks before it was a question. Disease: the access layer writing records nobody reads (Ch. 4). Fix: the security glance's silhouettes, evidence before conversation, facts routed without verdicts.</p><p><b>Pattern 5 — the storage surprise.</b> Symptom: the ceiling hit mid-quarter; uploads failing at the counter; the upgrade negotiated under duress; a year of announcements unread. Disease: the waistline never on a line (Ch. 5). Fix: the monthly dot, bends decomposed, misuse patterns met by design conversations, capacity engaged a year ahead.</p><p><b>Pattern 6 — slowness accepted as weather.</b> Symptom: 'the system is slow' as ambient truth; no timings, no questions, no findings; the complaint chronic and the diagnosis never made. Disease: feelings never converted to measurements (Ch. 6). Fix: the three questions, the timing habit, the report discipline, and the honest routing — habits fixed at the site, capacity cases made with evidence.</p><p><b>The seventh law.</b> The custodian, the fences, the masters, the survival, the evolution, the voice — and now the watching: <b>the system is read before it is felt — the error log by shape, the scheduler as every calendar's heartbeat, the doors by silhouette with evidence before conversation, the waistline monthly, slowness measured into findings, and the whole assembled weekly onto one page whose thresholds act — because the read costs minutes and the felt costs mornings.</b> The un-watched site has no fewer problems; it has later, louder ones. Module 8 closes the track — and the academy's seventh certification — with the structural layer: security, audit, and the administration that survives its administrator.</p>"),
]

